 types  and in immune cell functions that want  to be targeted  and most notably here we can think about  macrophages  and that particularly their function  their role in  tumor cell clearance is really taking a  lot of interest at the moment  and a lot of that interest is actually  being uh driven  by er of weissman at the  stanford university in california  and this quotation really  really nicely exemplifies some of the  challenge  faced when thinking about this is a  novel therapy  i'll read it out to you macrophage media  cytotoxicity occurs primarily via  phagocytosis  which is technically challenging to  assay and requires microscopic  visualization or flow cytometry  to quantify cellular engulfment for  these reasons macrophages  are underappreciated as effector cells  that can target cancer  and this really sets us a nice backdrop  to say  how we feel that real time light cell  analysis  can really aid researchers in this field  and really enable  i think novel therapies within uh  immunotherapy  before going into the specifics of the  phagocytosis application  i wanted to really talk about some of  the general key features of live cell  analysis systems  and here we've just got six of those  exemplified  firstly we've got automated real-time  analysis  it's really key to be able to do simple  hands-free  analysis such that you can gain the full  kinetic response of the biology that  you're interested in studying  and by doing this in such a simple way  you can get  avoid the need for complex sample  preparation for readouts  and importantly not have to pre-define  endpoints when you actually want to take  your measurements  by using image-based methodologies  and you're really able to understand the  spatial relationship  between different cell types key when  you're looking at  co-culture models and once you've been  able to  make measurements you can go back and  provide qualitative  validation of those measurements  to gain additional confidence  flexibility is always key  both in terms of optics readouts  the plastic wear and importantly the  cell types that you can use  we want the researchers to be able to  use whatever cells they feel  are most appropriate for their  application  being able to multiplex multiple  readouts within the same well  allows you to ensure that every data  point is  as information rich as possible  one of the nice things around live cell  analysis  is it not only has its place within  assays it actually um fits to the entire  continuum  of the live cell assays portfolio  whether that's  cell culture cell manipulation as well  as assays  and importantly at the end of the such  an approach the cells are not exhausted  they're still free to be used  in more traditional endpoint  measurements  when considering the use of live cells  it's always  critical that they're not perturbed  because you if your  assay methodology perturbs them it's  liable to interfere with the readout  to achieve this you need exquisite  environmental control  particularly crucial when looking at  longer term assays  our approach and philosophy is if you  can put your methodology  inside a standard cell culture incubator  where you have excellent control of  temperature carbon dioxide and  humidity this really gives you the  maximum chance of being non-perturbing  and then finally thinking about  non-perturbance  we want capacity and throughput without  affecting the biology  and if you can do this by running  different experiments in parallel  both for multiple vessels and multiple  users this really maximizes  the capability of such an approach  and finally being able to actually bring  together  all of these features of live cell  analysis with both non-perturbing  reagents  and fully validated lifestyle protocols  really maximizes the ability to have a  fully integrated approach  so specifically thinking about the  phagocytosis application  firstly i'd like to describe the  application principle  we have developed our own reagent the  inky site frodo  red sable red labeling kit  whereby we can label target cells  with a ph sensitive probe which when  those cells are in  standard media outside of the phagocyte  they have little or no fluorescence  but once they become engulfed by the  phagocyte  they enter the phagosome and ultimately  the favored lysosome  which has a much lower ph and we can  exploit that low ph  by using this ph sensitive probe which  will  increase dramatically its fluorescence  when in that environment  and if you can see the video the movie  running on the right hand side  you can see the larger elongated  macrophage  engulfing these smaller target cells  and once they've become engulfed there's  an increase in red fluorescence within  that macrophage and that is  fundamentally what we want to measure  in terms of how we go about conducting  such an assay  we've got the protocol and workflow  shown here which requires a few simple  steps  step one you have to seed your effect to  cell  which is your phagocyte and in most  cases will be a macrophage  once they've had the opportunity to  adhere to the plastic which is usually  overnight  you can go ahead and label your target  cells this is achieved  by creating a cell suspension  which in turn is then the photo label is  added  incubated for approximately hour the  cells are then washed  during that incubation period at step  three we can  treat our effector cells if that's what  we would wish to do  and then at step four we create the co  culture where those labeled target cells  are added to the effector cells and then  the  plate can be added to the live cell  imaging platform  for full analysis  by lowering  all of these features the reagent  the real-time analysis software and the  hardware imaging platform  you can again really get to see the  fully integrated solution  prior to actually demonstrating  validation  of the application i wanted to highlight  the step first is to actually validate  